---
// X-case-{id} — THE case-study template. One template, five routes (DSGN-02).
//
// ONE PAGE PER CASE. The user's direction was "one page per case, not like
// scroll to 10 cases in single page scrolling down".
// `00-RESPONSIVE-CONTRACT.md` §7 reads that as ROUTING rather than length — a
// case may still scroll internally, it just may not share a scroll with four
// others — and supersedes D-39 while it is there. R-3 resolves the route shape
// as `/work/{id}` over `/case/{id}`: a case study is evidence *under* Work, and
// putting it there makes the Work page the index of its own children instead of
// a page that links sideways into an unrelated top-level section.
//
// This file replaces `src/pages/case/long.astro` and `src/pages/case/short.astro`,
// which stacked two and three studies on one scroll respectively. Those two are
// exactly what the direction rules out. Almost everything else survives them: the
// five Case*.astro components, case.css, the 68ch measure, the 200px mono rail,
// the gap-block --wire treatment, the figure treatment and the three
// non-final-content treatments are unchanged. What is gone is the tier split and
// the stacking.
//
// NO TIERS. There is one composition and one length band. hued, Momentum and
// TimeShift used to render through a "short" template asserting they had NO
// numbered register; the design system and Cairn through a "long" one asserting
// they DID. That assertion pair is deleted rather than merged: a register is now
// a property of a study, not a class of study, and the evidence that the single
// template holds is that a three-entry register and a running section come out of
// the identical composition. The register count is REPORTED on the page (see
// `registerEntries` below) so a reviewer can see which is which without the
// template caring.
//
// THE PROSE IS READ IN PLACE FROM THE COMMITTED DRAFTS. Nothing is duplicated
// into this gitignored directory. src/lib/copy.mjs reads
// .planning/phases/00-design-ideation/00-COPY/*.md at build time and writes
// nothing, so the sketch and the draft cannot drift. The drafts survive this
// directory's deletion; this file does not.
//
// ZERO FRAMEWORK JAVASCRIPT. Every component composed here — Heading, Text,
// Eyebrow, Divider, Badge — is hook-free, so Astro renders it to static HTML.
// There is no hydration directive anywhere in this file or in the five case
// components, and check-no-js.sh gates it across every built route. The one
// interactive thing on the page, the review toggle, is a checkbox and a CSS
// selector.
//
// A GREP DEPENDS ON THIS FILE. The acceptance criteria count hydration
// directives here, so the name of that directive is never spelled out in this
// file, in prose or in a comment. Plans 01, 04, 07 and 09 each rediscovered that
// a grep cannot tell a rule from a sentence describing one; this paragraph is the
// tripwire so the next author does not rediscover it a sixth time.

import { Eyebrow, Text } from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import CaseChrome from "../../components/CaseChrome.astro";
import CaseFigure from "../../components/CaseFigure.astro";
import CaseHeader from "../../components/CaseHeader.astro";
import CaseProse from "../../components/CaseProse.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Public from "../../layouts/Public.astro";
import { CASE_STUDIES } from "../../lib/artefacts.mjs";
import { leadNotes, loadOneLiners, loadStudy, spansText } from "../../lib/copy.mjs";

import "../../styles/case.css";

// THE MEASURE, DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN ONLY IN THE STYLESHEET.
//
// 68ch. UI-SPEC derives it: at --text-md 15px DM Sans, 68ch is about 620px,
// which sits inside the standard 55-75ch readability band. It is written as a
// literal in the artefact so the derived number is visible where it is used
// rather than buried in a shared file, and CaseProse asserts the declared value
// matches its own canonical one. That assertion used to guard two templates
// against drifting apart; with one template it guards this file against drifting
// from the stylesheet, which is the same failure with one fewer place to happen.
const MEASURE = "68ch";

// ── The five routes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE IDS ARE D-38's, AND THEY ARE THE ROUTE. One array, imported rather than
// declared here, read by getStaticPaths — so there is no hand-listed URL set
// anywhere: a sixth study cannot be added to the corpus without a route
// appearing, and a route cannot exist for a study with no draft, because
// loadStudy throws on a missing file.
//
// It is imported from artefacts.mjs for two reasons, both in that file's own
// comment: the registry asserts every id there has a matching `X-case-*`
// artefact, and Astro hoists `getStaticPaths` above the frontmatter, so a local
// `const` would be in the temporal dead zone when it runs.

// ── D-41's asset plan, per study ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A hero plus one or two inline screenshots "where a decision is easier shown
// than described". That is a claim about a POSITION in the argument, not about a
// page having pictures, so each inline slot names the register entry it belongs
// under and CaseProse asserts the ordinal exists. Every caption and every source
// below is taken from the study's own ## Assets section — this page renders the
// asset plan the draft states rather than inventing one.
//
// `section: MIDDLE` IS A SENTINEL, NOT A HEADING. It resolves at render time to
// whichever of MIDDLE_HEADINGS that particular draft used. Writing "Decisions"
// here would work today and would silently drop the figure from any draft
// spelling it "Decision" — the figure list is a filter, so a section name that
// matches nothing yields no figure and no error. That is the same class of silent
// drop the loader exists to prevent, one layer up, and it is what plan 00-20's
// positive control exercises end to end.
const MIDDLE = "@middle";

const ASSETS: Record<string, any[]> = {
	"design-system": [
		{
			role: "Hero",
			hero: true,
			ratio: "16 / 9",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"This site, rendered — a wide capture of a real page from this portfolio. It is the one image that makes the study's closing claim checkable rather than rhetorical.",
			source: "this portfolio, live",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 1",
			section: MIDDLE,
			afterEntry: "1",
			ratio: "16 / 9",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The same component in both themes, side by side. Decision 1 is a claim about a defect that existed in only one theme — light mode set --ink-4 and --ink-3 to the identical value — so a paired light/dark capture shows in one glance why reviewing in a single mode could never have caught it.",
			source: "Storybook — design-system-ed1.pages.dev",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 2",
			section: MIDDLE,
			afterEntry: "3",
			ratio: "16 / 10",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The Storybook a11y panel on a story, reporting zero warnings. Decision 3 is about enforcement rather than intention, and a screenshot of the check passing is more convincing than the sentence claiming it does.",
			source: "Storybook — design-system-ed1.pages.dev",
		},
	],
	cairn: [
		{
			role: "Hero",
			hero: true,
			ratio: "16 / 9",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The board, populated — the kanban across its five stages. It establishes the product in one image and makes the pipeline decision legible without a paragraph explaining it.",
			source: "cairn.co.in, live",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 1",
			section: MIDDLE,
			afterEntry: "1",
			ratio: "3 / 4",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The Rejection Reframe — the single screen the worst-week test was written for, and the one place where the anti-gamification stance is a visible design choice rather than an absence. It is the shipped counter-example to the confetti every other tracker would show here.",
			source: "cairn.co.in, live",
		},
	],
	hued: [
		{
			role: "Hero",
			hero: true,
			ratio: "1024 / 500",
			inRepo: true,
			caption:
				"publishing/feature-graphic.png — it already carries the palette strip as its whole composition, and therefore states the idea without a caption.",
			source: "../hued/publishing/feature-graphic.png",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 1",
			section: MIDDLE,
			afterEntry: null,
			ratio: "9 / 16",
			inRepo: true,
			caption:
				"publishing/screenshots/final/01.png — the launch state, showing a real clustered palette, which is the output the CIELAB decision above is about. Six finished screenshots exist in that directory; D-41 caps this page at the hero plus one, so the rest stay unused.",
			source: "../hued/publishing/screenshots/final/01.png",
		},
	],
	momentum: [
		{
			role: "Hero",
			hero: true,
			ratio: "1024 / 500",
			inRepo: true,
			caption: "store-listing/feature-graphic.png — the one composed asset the repository carries.",
			source: "../Momentum/store-listing/feature-graphic.png",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 1",
			section: "Problem",
			afterEntry: null,
			ratio: "9 / 16",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"A goal card with the day's required amount on it. That number is the argument the whole page makes and it is easier shown than described. Not in the repository — store-listing/ holds only the feature graphic, the launcher icon, mock data and a local database file.",
			source: "the live Play Store listing",
		},
	],
	timeshift: [
		{
			role: "Hero",
			hero: true,
			ratio: "16 / 9",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The right-click conversion happening on a real page — that single moment is the product and it needs no caption. Not in the repository: the only image the repo carries is icons/icon512.png, which is the extension icon and cannot stand in for it.",
			source: "the live Chrome Web Store listing",
		},
		{
			role: "Inline 1",
			section: MIDDLE,
			afterEntry: null,
			ratio: "16 / 10",
			inRepo: false,
			caption:
				"The resolver reporting medium confidence on an ambiguous abbreviation — the half of that decision easier shown than described.",
			source: "the live Chrome Web Store listing",
		},
	],
};

// ── Build ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return CASE_STUDIES.map((meta) => ({ params: { id: meta.id }, props: { meta } }));
}

const { meta } = Astro.props;

const study = loadStudy(`case-${meta.id}`);
const oneLiners = loadOneLiners();

// The draft's H1 may carry a drafting suffix, so the page title comes from the
// canonical project title and the H1 is asserted to START with it. That keeps the
// two from drifting without forcing every draft to be retitled.
if (!study.title.startsWith(meta.title)) {
	throw new Error(
		`X-case-${meta.id}: ${study.file} opens with the H1 "${study.title}", which does not start ` +
			`with the canonical project title "${meta.title}". Reconcile the draft or the title map.`,
	);
}

const canonical = oneLiners[meta.project];
if (!canonical?.oneLiner || !canonical?.badge) {
	throw new Error(
		`X-case-${meta.id}: 00-COPY/one-liners.md has no "## ${meta.project}" entry carrying both a ` +
			"one-liner and a badge. That file is canonical for both — it is the only copy in the " +
			"corpus with an enforced character budget, and X-work and X-home-act2 are laid out " +
			"against those exact strings.",
	);
}

// Where both declare a D-45 status they must agree, or two artefacts are quietly
// telling a reader different things about whether a project is current.
if (study.frontmatter.badge && study.frontmatter.badge !== canonical.badge) {
	throw new Error(
		`X-case-${meta.id}: ${study.file} declares \`badge: ${study.frontmatter.badge}\` while ` +
			`00-COPY/one-liners.md declares "${canonical.badge}" for ${meta.project}.`,
	);
}

// The sentinel resolved. One line, and it is the whole either-branch at this
// layer: whichever spelling this draft used is the section the figures attach to.
const assets = (ASSETS[meta.id] ?? []).map((a) =>
	a.section === MIDDLE ? { ...a, section: study.middleHeading } : a,
);

// D-41 is a budget, not a suggestion: a hero plus ONE OR TWO inline screenshots.
// Asserted rather than trusted, because the budget is what stops a case study
// drifting into a gallery — and because an extra inline slot added by hand would
// change the page's vertical rhythm, which is the thing under review here.
//
// ONE BUDGET, NOT TWO. The old short template allowed at most one inline and the
// long template required at least one; that split was D-39's and D-39 is gone.
// The surviving range is D-41's own.
const heroes = assets.filter((a) => a.hero).length;
const inlineCount = assets.length - heroes;
if (heroes !== 1 || inlineCount < 1 || inlineCount > 2) {
	throw new Error(
		`X-case-${meta.id}: ${meta.title}'s asset plan is ${heroes} hero and ${inlineCount} inline ` +
			"figure(s). D-41 allows exactly one hero plus one or two inline screenshots.",
	);
}

// Reported, not asserted. Two of the five studies carry a numbered register and
// three carry a running section; that difference used to select a template and
// now selects nothing. Printing it on the page is what makes "the identical
// composition carries both" checkable by a reviewer rather than claimed.
const registerEntries = study.sections[study.middleHeading].filter((b: any) => b.kind === "h3" && b.ordinal).length;

// THE ONE CLOSING THAT MUST NOT BE TRUNCATED.
//
// The design-system study ends with a paragraph pointing at the page the reader
// is on: it is the only project whose outcome the reader is looking at while
// reading about it, and D-38 makes that the reason it is the flagship. That
// paragraph sits AFTER the study's gap block, and it is finished prose rather
// than a placeholder.
//
// The distinction is fragile enough to be worth a build assertion. The corpus
// checker measures a gap as everything from the marker to the next heading, which
// is the right rule for a length FLOOR and would, applied to rendering, swallow
// this paragraph and render it muted and labelled provisional — a false statement
// about the only claim in the corpus that is already true. The loader therefore
// scopes a gap opened inside a block quote to that block quote. This asserts the
// consequence rather than trusting it.
if (meta.id === "design-system") {
	const outcome = study.sections.Outcome;
	const lastProse = [...outcome].reverse().find((b: any) => b.kind !== "note");
	if (!lastProse || lastProse.kind !== "p" || !spansText(lastProse.spans).includes("the page you are reading")) {
		throw new Error(
			"X-case-design-system: the study's ## Outcome must END with its page-pointing closing " +
				"paragraph as ordinary prose, not inside the gap block.\n" +
				`  Last non-note block is "${lastProse ? lastProse.kind : "(none)"}".\n` +
				"  If the gap boundary rule changed, that paragraph is now rendering as provisional — " +
				"which would be wrong about the one outcome in the corpus that needs no interview.",
		);
	}
}

// The corpus index, rendered in the chrome. Every study links to its own route,
// so a reader who lands on one case can reach the other four without going back
// through /work — and, more to the point, every route is reachable from every
// other route, which is what stops five separate pages being five orphans.
const siblings = CASE_STUDIES.map((s) => ({
	anchor: s.id,
	href: `/work/${s.id}/`,
	label: s.title,
	current: s.id === meta.id,
	words: s.id === meta.id ? study.counts.bandWords : null,
}));

const LEGEND = [
	{
		term: "[NEEDS AKHIL]",
		body: "A D-40 gap: placeholder prose written at finished length, because a six-word stub would hand this template a measure the real copy will never fit. Rendered at paragraph scale behind a solid rule so a reviewer can see that a paragraph-scale gap occupies a paragraph-scale slot. Filled in the final-phase interview. Never stripped. All five studies carry exactly one, in the same place — no repository records what happened after a thing was built.",
	},
	{
		term: "[source: …]",
		body: "Provenance. Every factual claim in these drafts names the file it was read out of, so a reviewer can audit rather than trust. Rendered inline in mono at the smallest step. Excluded from the R-1 word count, so a compression pass cannot reach the band by keeping its citations and cutting its sentences. Phase 6 strips them, and that removal is a planned step rather than something to remember.",
	},
	{
		term: "Drafting note",
		body: "An HTML comment in the draft, carrying what was searched and why it came up empty, a correction to a stale figure in a project's own README, or what a compression pass cut and why. Offset in mono so it never reads as prose. Phase 6 strips these too, but the corrections have to survive the stripping.",
	},
	{
		term: "Reserved figure",
		body: "A D-41 slot at its intended aspect ratio, holding the space an image will take. No image is rendered even where the file exists: hued is the only project whose assets are committed, and rendering its two for real while the rest stayed reserved would make one study look different from the others for a reason that has nothing to do with the design. That is D-42's CLS argument made visible before a single byte exists.",
	},
];

const HEADLINE = `${meta.title} — the case-study template at one route per study`;

const PROVES =
	`That ONE template renders this study at a 68ch measure with nothing bespoke to it. ` +
	`This route carries ${registerEntries > 0 ? `a ${registerEntries}-entry numbered register` : "a running decision section with no register"}, ` +
	`${inlineCount === 1 ? "one inline figure" : `${inlineCount} inline figures`} and a paragraph-scale gap block, ` +
	`in ${study.counts.bandWords} words over its four required sections — inside R-1's 500-700 band, which the loader ` +
	`asserts at build time for all five studies. The other four routes differ from this one only in their copy: same ` +
	`stylesheet, same components, same measure, same spacing, nothing tightened or loosened per study. Every word ` +
	`below is the committed first-pass draft, read in place at build time — nothing here is lorem, and nothing here ` +
	`is a six-word stub standing in for a paragraph.`;
---

<Public title={`X-case-${meta.id} — ${meta.title} case study`} active="work" maxWidth={980}>
	<div class="case-page" style={`--case-measure: ${MEASURE};`}>
		<CaseChrome
			artefact={`X-case-${meta.id}`}
			headline={HEADLINE}
			proves={PROVES}
			measure={MEASURE}
			studies={siblings}
			legend={LEGEND}
		/>

		<article class="case-study">
			<CaseHeader
				anchor={meta.id}
				tierLabel="Case study"
				title={meta.title}
				badge={canonical.badge}
				oneLiner={canonical.oneLiner}
				meta={[
					study.file,
					`/work/${meta.id}/`,
					`${study.frontmatter.status} · awaiting ${study.frontmatter.awaiting}`,
					`${study.counts.bandWords.toLocaleString("en-US")} words (R-1: 500-700)`,
					`${study.counts.sources} sourced claims`,
					`${study.counts.gaps} gap block`,
					`## ${study.middleHeading} · ${registerEntries || "no"} register ${registerEntries === 1 ? "entry" : "entries"}`,
				]}
			/>

			{/* The draft's own preamble is an instruction to this template rather
			    than prose for the page — it says the study opens with the canonical
			    one-liner, which the header above does. It renders as a drafting note
			    so that nothing in the committed draft is silently dropped, and so a
			    reviewer can see the instruction beside the thing that carried it
			    out. Three of the five drafts open with an HTML comment instead, and
			    those pass through as notes unchanged. */}
			<div class="case-section">
				<div class="case-rail"><span class="case-rail-ord">00</span><span>preamble</span></div>
				<div class="case-col">
					<CaseProse
						blocks={leadNotes(study)}
						measure={MEASURE}
						where={`X-case-${meta.id} / preamble`}
					/>
				</div>
			</div>

			{assets.filter((a) => a.hero).map((a) => <CaseFigure {...a} />)}

			{study.required.map((heading: string, i: number) => (
				<CaseSection
					index={i + 1}
					total={study.required.length}
					heading={heading}
					blocks={study.sections[heading]}
					measure={MEASURE}
					figures={assets.filter((a) => !a.hero && a.section === heading)}
					where={`X-case-${meta.id} / ## ${heading}`}
					last={i === study.required.length - 1}
				/>
			))}
		</article>

		<footer class="case-section">
			<div class="case-rail">
				<span class="case-rail-ord">END</span>
			</div>
			<div class="case-col">
				<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">THE OTHER FOUR</Eyebrow>
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted" leading="relaxed">
					One study per route, five routes. R-3 puts them under Work rather than at
					`/case/{`{id}`}` because a case study is evidence <em>under</em> Work, which makes
					the Work page the index of its own children instead of a page linking sideways.
					The stacked `/case/long` and `/case/short` routes this replaces are retired: they
					put two and three studies on one scroll, which is the arrangement the direction
					rules out.
				</Text>
				<ul class="case-index case-index-end">
					<li><a href="/work">← Work — all five projects</a></li>
					{
						siblings
							.filter((s) => !s.current)
							.map((s) => (
								<li>
									<a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a>
								</li>
							))
					}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</footer>
	</div>
</Public>
